{
  "corpus": "guard-v1",
  "corpus_version": "v2.0",
  "frozen_by": "Corpus v2, frozen at M3 node P's start by the operator's Batch Approval on the batch P-B1 pull request (MianliWang/gatebraid); the freeze commit is that batch's merge commit, recorded in the approval and in fixtures/CORPUS-v2-PLAN.md section 4. Fixtures precede the tool: this corpus exists before bin/gatebraid-guard.py and bin/gatebraid-doctor.py are written (M3-PLAN.md section 2 P).",
  "description": "One negative case per guard check (twelve checks, M3-PLAN.md section 2 P), frictions #92 and #106 entering as cases, four structural cases, and the positive arm of every negative. Each negative is a gatebraid/guard-verdict@1 document that reports a violating fact beside a passing result -- what a correct guard can never emit. The instrument half of every item (that the guard extracts these facts correctly from raw host readings) freezes where its instrument exists: Slice P2-S7 replays each case's facts through the guard and the verdict must equal the recorded one (ADR-0031 decision 2's pattern; spec section 4).",
  "loader": "CPython 3.11.15 (/usr/bin/python3), jsonschema 4.26.0, Draft202012Validator; measured by the coordinator in the sandbox; the landing captures record the host loaders",
  "known_limitation": "Two relations are named rather than left looking enforced. (1) The schema binds facts to results; it cannot bind raw host readings to facts. A guard that misreads the host and reports clean facts emits a valid document; that class is caught only by the instrument half at P2-S7 (recorded raw readings replayed, verdict compared). (2) The twelve coverage clauses assert that each check id appears at least once among exactly twelve entries, which together force exactly once; no clause asserts the order.",
